<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albert I of Belgium</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1875">1875</a>–<a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as King Albert I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albert I was king of the Belgians from 1909 to 1934. He came to the throne a year after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> as the Belgian Congo, which he visited as heir in 1909, and supported reform of its administration. He led the Belgian army against the German invasion in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-i-of-belgium.jpg" alt="Albert I of Belgium" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard Speaight · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Albert_I_of_Belgium_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Albert was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> on 8 April 1875, the fifth child and second son of Prince Philippe, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, and Princess Marie of Hohenzollern-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmaringen">Sigmaringen</a>. He grew up in the Palace of the Count of Flanders, at first third in line to the throne. When his older brother Baudouin, who had been prepared for the crown, died young, he unexpectedly became second in line at the age of 16. Retiring and studious, he prepared strenuously for kingship, and in his youth he travelled incognito round working-class districts to observe living conditions.</p><p>On 2 October 1900 he married Elisabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>. They had three children: Leopold, later Leopold III, Charles, and Marie-José, last Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The couple cultivated friendships with scientists, artists and musicians and made their court at Laeken a kind of cultural salon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Albert succeeded his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a> on 23 December 1909 and took the accession oath in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> as well as French, an innovation. He and Elisabeth were popular for a simple lifestyle that contrasted with Leopold II's aloof, autocratic manner. Shortly before accession he had toured the Belgian Congo, annexed in 1908, found it in poor condition and recommended reforms to protect the native population.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> was under harsh German occupation. Albert worked through secret diplomatic channels for a negotiated peace without victors or vanquished, but neither <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> nor the Entente favoured it. At the end of the war he commanded Army Group Flanders of Belgian, British and French divisions in the final offensive that liberated Belgium. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a> in April 1919 he sought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> and security, and advised against a harsh treaty with Germany, but his advice had little influence. In 1918 he formed a Government of National Union of Catholics, Liberals and Socialists and helped bring about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-suffrage">universal male suffrage</a>. In 1920 he changed the family name from Saxe-Coburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> to House of Belgium.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>A passionate alpinist, Albert died on 17 February 1934 climbing alone on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoffmann-la-roche">Roche</a> du Vieux Bon Dieu at Marche-les-Dames, near Namur. Official investigations gave two possible explanations: a boulder he leaned on came loose, or the pinnacle holding his rope broke, so that he fell about 18 metres. Rumours of murder were dismissed by most historians, and DNA testing of bloodstained leaves in 2016 concluded he had died at that spot. He was buried in the Royal Crypt at the Church of Our Lady of Laeken and succeeded by Leopold III. In 1925, influenced by Carl E. Akeley, he founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>'s first national park, now Virunga National Park. Belgians call him the Knight King or Soldier King.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Albert I of Belgium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In August 1914 Germany demanded passage for its armies, and when <b>King Albert I</b>’s government refused, Germany invaded on 4 August, bringing Britain into the First World War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Kingdom of Belgium · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Palace of the Count of Flanders, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. Died in Rochers de Marche-les-Dames, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.84,4.36&amp;year=1913" aria-label="Palace of the Count of Flanders on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4649 400 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Albert I of Belgium"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5099 670h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5099 670h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.84,4.36&amp;year=1913">What was here in 1913? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
